# The Plot - a sexton, a leaning stone, and a burial on Thursday.
# Written for the library. One person, one rule meant for safety, and a family that has to live with it.

title: The Plot
start: start

== start ==
bg cliff_path
music room_tone
The burial is Thursday at eleven and the grave is open, and the soil is going on the
sheet beside it the way it always does, turf first and then the rest, and the turf goes
back on the mound when it is filled.
You have dug a hundred and forty of these. You can dig one in a day and a half and you
have never once been late, and the vicar says that at funerals the way he says the
weather, which is to say without meaning much by it.
-> the_stone

== the_stone ==
While the grave is open and the light is on it, you walk the row to check for anything
that has come loose over the winter, because that is the other half of the job and it is
the half that has a rule attached.
The stone by the gate has come loose. It is a kerb and a headstone for a woman who died
in 1958, it belongs to a family that still uses the churchyard, and it is leaning twelve
degrees off plumb with a base that has washed out under it.
-> the_rule

== the_rule ==
The rule is the diocese's and it is two sentences long and it is about safety: a
memorial that is found unstable is made safe, and the usual way to make it safe is to
lay it down flat on the ground, because a stone lying down cannot fall on a child.
There is a test they teach you with a hand on the top and a steady push, and this one
moves, and it moves at the base rather than the joint, which is the worst of the two.
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
bg workshop_bench
You can fix it properly. You have done it twice: lift the stone, pour a new footing,
set it back plumb, and pin the joints with stainless dowels, and it will stand for
another sixty years. It takes a day and it costs about ninety pounds in materials, and
nobody in this story has ninety pounds.
Laying it flat takes forty minutes and costs nothing and is, by the letter of the rule,
the correct answer.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
It is Tuesday. The burial is Thursday, so the family will be here on Thursday, standing
four rows away from the stone with their coats on, and they will see whatever you have
done to it, and they will not know there was a decision.
* Lay it flat. It is the rule and it is forty minutes. -> flat
* Fix it properly, on your own time and your own money. -> fix_it
* Ring the family and ask them what they want. -> ring_them
* Leave it up and report it, so it is the church's decision and not yours. -> report_it

== flat ==
You lay it flat. Forty minutes, and the stone goes down onto a bed of sand and the
kerbs come up and go into the corner of the wall with the others, and you stand back
and the row looks tidier than it did, which is the part nobody tells you.
On Thursday the family comes and buries the second wife of the man in that grave, and
they walk past the flat stone at the gate without stopping, and you are standing at the
door of the shed with a spade and you watch them not stop.
-> ending_flat

== fix_it ==
You lift it on the Wednesday and you pour the footing and you set it back plumb and the
pin comes out of the kit you keep for the church's own gates, so it costs you the
footing and the sand and the afternoon, which is less than ninety pounds and is still
not nothing.
It stands. On Thursday the family comes, and one of them, an older woman, stops at the
gate on the way out and puts her hand flat on the top of the stone and reads it, and
then goes on, and you are forty feet away with a spade and you do not say anything.
-> ending_fixed

== ring_them ==
The number is in the register from the last burial in that plot, nineteen years ago, and
it rings and a man answers and you tell him what you can see and what the rule is and
what the two options are.
He asks how much the proper one is. You tell him. He says he will ring you back and he
does not ring you back, and on Thursday the family comes and the stone is still standing
and leaning and nobody says anything about it and nobody touches it.
-> ending_asked

== report_it ==
You write it in the log and you ring the churchwarden, which is the other thing the rule
allows, and she comes on the Thursday morning in her coat to look at it before the
burial and stands there with her hands in her pockets.
churchwarden: It's the base.
you: It's the base.
churchwarden: If I say lay it down, then it's me that said it.
you: Yes.
She looks at it for a while longer and then she says lay it down, and you lay it down on
the Friday, and it is her name in the log and not yours.
-> ending_report

== ending_flat ==
The rule is a good rule. A stone lying down does not fall on a child, and the churchyard
is a place children walk through, and you have made safe eleven stones in nine years and
none of them has hurt anybody.
It is also true that you did it on a Tuesday and they came on a Thursday, and that the
stone is the only thing in the world with that woman's name on it apart from a line in a
book, and that it is now lying in the grass with the letters facing the sky, where the
weather will have them in twenty years. You did the right thing in the wrong order.
-> END

== ending_fixed ==
Nobody knows. That is the whole of it: the warden does not know, the vicar does not know,
the family does not know, and the only evidence is a footing that is newer than the rest
of the concrete in that row and a pin out of the kit.
You walk past it for years. It is plumb, and it is going to stay plumb, and if anybody
ever asks you will tell them, and nobody is ever going to ask, and a thing nobody asks
about is a thing that did not happen.
-> END

== ending_asked ==
He never rings back. You think about ringing him again about eleven times over the
following fortnight and you do not, because it is his stone and his father's wife and his
decision, and because a person who does not ring back has rung back, and you did not
need nine years of churchyard to know it.
The stone is still standing. You check it every month for a year, and it does not move
any further, and then you stop checking it every month and check it every spring like
everything else.
-> END

== ending_report ==
Her name is in the log. That is what the log is for and it is the whole reason the rule
has a second sentence, which is that the person who makes the decision signs for it.
She was not avoiding it, that is not what happened - she stood in the cold and looked at
it and took it, and then she went to the burial, and she will carry it, and that is what
a warden is.
You lay it down on the Friday. You do not think about it again, which is the other thing
the log is for.
-> END
